            <ArticleTitle>Increased frequency of Epstein-Barr virus excretion in patients with new daily persistent headaches.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>411-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a case-control study 27 (84%) of 32 patients with new daily persistent headaches (NDPH) and 8 (25%) of 32 controls had evidence of Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) &quot;active&quot; infection, as demonstrated by EBV excretion and/or early antigen titre above 1:32. 20 (62%) patients and 4 (12%) controls were excreting EBV in the oropharynx, as determined by a dot hybridisation assay. The mean titre of IgG antibodies to early antigen was significantly higher in patients than controls. EBV reactivation may be important in the pathogenesis of NDPH. Alternatively, patients with NDPH may be unusually prone to EBV reactivation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
